WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.520
When it comes to the blockbuster success of
the Marvel Cinematic Universe, there's a ton

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:07.560
of factors that have built the franchise into
what it is today.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:13.289
But it was the 1998 release of Blade, a gory
action movie packed with leather coats and

00:00:13.289 --> 00:00:17.650
thrilling set pieces, that first broke the
comic movie mold in the nineties and showed

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:21.870
longtime readers and mainstream audiences
alike that Marvel Comics could be cool.

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:27.320
But the 1998 release of Blade isn't the whole
story; in fact, there's a lot you might not

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.300
know about your favorite bloodsucker-busting
hero.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:33.130
Here's the untold truth of Blade.

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:35.089
Tomb of Dracula

00:00:35.089 --> 00:00:40.909
Blade was created by Gene Colan and Marv Wolfman
in 1973 during their run on the Tomb of Dracula

00:00:40.909 --> 00:00:45.499
series, which followed a group of characters
tasked with killing the Lord of Vampires.

00:00:45.499 --> 00:00:49.999
It wasn't just a horror comic, however; the
series brought Dracula directly into the Marvel

00:00:49.999 --> 00:00:53.649
Universe with crossovers and superpowered
guest stars.

00:00:53.649 --> 00:00:57.929
Readers eventually learned that Blade's real
name was Eric Brooks, and that he had received

00:00:57.929 --> 00:01:02.770
both his strength and his hatred for vampires
from his mother, who was bitten by a vampire

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:04.360
while she was giving birth to him.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:10.050
The event gave him immunity to vampirism,
as well as a prolonged lifespan and fangs.

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:14.530
After Tomb of Dracula's final issue, Blade
popped up now and again in the Marvel Universe

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:16.930
whenever a title needed a dose of horror.

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:18.041
Blade: The Movie

00:01:18.041 --> 00:01:24.000
It was the release of Blade in 1998 that catapulted
the character to new creative heights.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.160
The movie reinvigorated the concept of a vampire-like
vampire hunter, showcasing a character that

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:34.360
lived in the shadows, killing sleazy vampires
in outlandish action sequences.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:38.780
Although the movie was directed by Steve Norrington,
producer and star Wesley Snipes deserves a

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:42.820
massive amount of credit for the character's
stature in pop culture history.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:47.320
Snipes would constantly adjust shots and ad-lib
certain lines of dialogue or scenes that,

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:50.500
while costly, were always beneficial to the
final cut.

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:53.840
Snipes even ad-libbed one of the most famous
lines of the movie:

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:57.820
"Some motherf----ers always trying to iceskate
uphill."

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:00.270
Blade 2

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:03.670
Blade was a smash success with an incredible
idea for a planned sequel.

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:07.270
"And at the end of the film, Blade and Karen
pick themselves up and there are hundreds

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:12.810
and hundreds of vampires out in the streets,
and it's become planet of the vampires."

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:16.610
Norrington turned down the opportunity to
direct the sequel, leaving the chair open

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:20.590
for Guillermo del Toro to step in for Blade
II in 2002.

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:24.610
The film followed Blade as he was forced to
team up with a group of specially trained

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:29.110
vampires to hunt a powerful mutant aberration
of vampires called "Reapers."

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:33.760
The film turned out to be a success, but it's
arguably most notable for featuring a murderer's

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:38.750
row of character actors who would go on to
major acclaim, including Norman Reedus, Donnie

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:40.760
Yen, and Ron Perlman.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:45.390
Critics were lukewarm, but the fans turned
out in force, turning Blade from a one-off

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:47.680
hit into a genuine franchise.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:50.110
Blade: Trinity

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:55.010
Blade II had moved the franchise forward,
but Blade: Trinity went off the rails.

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:59.100
Directed by series writer David Goyer, it
intended to bring in more of the classic elements

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:03.510
from the comics, like vampire detective Hannibal
King and Dracula.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:07.520
Unfortunately, by all accounts, it was a disaster
for everyone involved.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:12.200
Patton Oswalt recalled his time on the set
with a series of stranger-than-fiction anecdotes

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:13.490
about Wesley Snipes.

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:18.230
"He would communicate with post-its that he
would give to the director.

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:20.580
And each one, he would sign 'Blade.'"

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:25.380
For his part, Snipes found the situation so
unpleasant that he filed a lawsuit against

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:29.959
Goyer, the producers, and New Line Cinema,
citing, among other things, the 'juvenile

00:03:29.959 --> 00:03:34.660
level of humor' in the screenplay as well
as the increased focus on Blade's sidekicks.

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:39.330
Blade: Trinity ended up being the worst reviewed
movie of the trilogy, and put a stake through

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:41.150
the heart of the franchise.

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:44.440
But as with any good vampire, Blade wouldn't
stay dead for long.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:45.440
[Intense music]

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:46.440
Blade TV

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:52.569
In 2006, Blade would return on the small screen
in Spike TV's Blade: The Series.

00:03:52.569 --> 00:03:56.770
Snipes was replaced by rapper Kirk "Sticky
Fingaz" Jones for the show, which continued

00:03:56.770 --> 00:03:58.120
the events of the movies.

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:02.010
While the show's expanding mythology seemed
intriguing, the series slowly hemorrhaged

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:06.660
viewers until the season finale — a slow
bleed that seemed oddly fitting for the vampire

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:08.030
show.

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:09.890
Blade in the Marvel Universe

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:13.950
While the Blade television show spelled the
end of the character's live action adaptations,

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:18.580
a new comic book was released less than a
week after the show's final episode aired.

00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:23.110
The series combined Blade's original comic
book origin with his vastly more popular movie

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:24.350
personality.

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:29.230
This new version of Blade kneecapped Spider-Man,
owed Wolverine a blood debt for saving his

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:33.440
life from a vampire years ago, and occasionally
did odd jobs for S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:37.800
While the comic only lasted twelve issues,
it did have one of the oddest team-ups in

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:38.960
Marvel history.

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:42.990
Dr. Doom kidnapped Blade to assist him in
saving an innocent life from vampires.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:44.230
The catch?

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:49.699
The victim was Dr. Doom's mother, a Romani
sorceress who was attacked by vampires decades

00:04:49.699 --> 00:04:53.840
ago... but was saved by Blade going back in
time to save her.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:57.620
Most importantly, the reasons Doom knew that
it was specifically Blade who saved his mother

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:01.500
was that he had perfect recall, even as a
fetus.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:02.500
Gross.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:03.500
Blade in Britain

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:08.110
Wesley Snipes' portrayal was so successful
that it's easy to forget Blade was born and

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:10.810
raised in London in the original comics.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:14.880
After the cancellation of the rebooted Blade
comic, he appeared in the ensemble cast of

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:20.190
Captain Britain and MI13, which followed a
group of British superheroes battling magical

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:21.280
threats.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:25.640
While on the team, Blade started a romantic
relationship with fellow team member Spitfire

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:30.460
— a vampire with superhuman speed who'd
been fighting her bloodlust since World War

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:31.460
II.

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:36.000
Having two vampires on the team turned out
to be a problem, though, when Dracula launched

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.580
an attack on Britain from his secret castle
on the moon.

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:44.400
Blade and the rest of MI13 were able to repel
Dracula's attack thanks to a captured Duke

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:49.919
of Hell, a mystical skull that kept vampires
from invading Great Britain, and the mystical

00:05:49.919 --> 00:05:50.919
sword of Excalibur.

00:05:50.919 --> 00:05:54.000
No one ever said this stuff was easy.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:55.580
Blade the Avenger

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:59.810
While Blade is most well known for fighting
vampires, he's often found himself at odds

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:02.830
with a wide variety of immortal beasts and
demons.

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:08.419
In The Mighty Avengers, this involved secretly
rallying a team of heroes to fight the Deathwalkers,

00:06:08.419 --> 00:06:11.710
four immortal rulers who each had dominion
over one of the elements.

00:06:11.710 --> 00:06:16.650
Intriguingly, this required Blade to adopt
disguises so as not to alert the Deathwalkers

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:23.150
— first as Spider-Hero, the non copyright-infringing
spider-themed hero of New York, and then as

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:27.639
Ronin, a catch-all ninja costume for superheroes
who need to go incognito.

00:06:27.639 --> 00:06:31.639
Don't worry, he eventually went back to his
trademark outfit.

00:06:31.639 --> 00:06:33.199
Return to television?

00:06:33.199 --> 00:06:37.669
While Blade might not currently have a comic
series, that doesn't mean Marvel's forgotten

00:06:37.669 --> 00:06:38.669
about him.

00:06:38.669 --> 00:06:42.711
In fact, there's a chance he might return
sooner than later — specifically, in an

00:06:42.711 --> 00:06:46.270
upcoming season of the ABC series Marvel's
Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:06:46.270 --> 00:06:51.860
A spokesperson for Marvel tweeted out about
the 2018 season with the hashtag #Blade, leading

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:57.220
fans to speculate that the show would incorporate
everyone's favorite vampire hunter.

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:01.610
Snipes even responded to tweets asking whether
he would be willing to reprise his role for

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:02.610
Marvel, saying

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:05.620
"When anyone asks 'Who could be the next Blade?'

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:08.419
you already know there's only one Blade fam!"

00:07:08.419 --> 00:07:10.430
Ball's in Marvel's court…

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:11.430
Thanks for watching!

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:13.830
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:15.889
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

